    "title": "Astronomical object",
    "pillar": "science",
    "explanation": "An astronomical object is any naturally occurring physical entity, association, or structure found in the universe. In everyday astronomy the words “object” and “body” are often used interchangeably, but they differ: a body is a single, tightly bound, contiguous physical thing, while an object can be a more complex, loosely bound structure made of multiple bodies or sub‑objects.\n\nExamples of objects include planetary systems, star clusters, nebulae, and galaxies; examples of bodies are asteroids, moons, planets, and stars. A comet illustrates the distinction: its frozen nucleus is a body, while the whole comet with a diffuse coma and tail is an object. This language helps clarify how astronomers describe scale and structure in space.",
